    erkennung: `Where the SE9 withdraws into coziness and the SO9 dissolves into the group, the SX9 seeks complete fusion with a single person. In my book <em>Enneagramm Zoo</em> I describe the image of the <strong>Sloth</strong>, hanging upside-down from a branch, bonding so thoroughly with its surroundings that the boundary between itself and the world begins to blur. She longs for unity, for a state without separation, and easily loses herself in the wishes, moods, and preferences of the person she loves. Anyone who wants to recognize her should watch for this gentle, almost dreamlike readiness to adapt: she seems relaxed and peaceful, but behind it often lies the quiet question of whether any will of her own is left at all – or whether it has long since dissolved into the other.`,
    wunde: { titel: "The wound behind it", text: `Behind this lies the <strong>wound of self-forgetting</strong> – the deep pattern of making oneself invisible in order to avoid conflict and preserve harmony. In the SX9, the self dissolves especially completely in fusion with the other: her own wishes and boundaries blur with those of the person she loves. The core belief behind it: "Only through connection with the other do I become whole" – underneath it: "If I set boundaries and want my own things, I lose the love and the unity."` },
